1️⃣1️⃣ Relative Speed ⭐⭐⭐

Relative speed tells us:

> How fast one object appears to move relative to another.




---

Objects Moving in Opposite Directions

Add speeds:

\[
\boxed{Relative\ Speed=S_1+S_2}
\]

Example:

A →→→ 60 km/h

B ←←← 40 km/h

Relative speed:

\[
60+40
\]

\[
\boxed{100\ km/h}
\]


---

1️⃣2️⃣ Same Direction

Subtract speeds:

\[
\boxed{Relative\ Speed=|S_1-S_2|}
\]

Example:

A →→→ 70 km/h
B →→→ 50 km/h

Relative speed:

\[
70-50
\]

\[
\boxed{20\ km/h}
\]


---

🧠 1️⃣3️⃣ Meeting Time

If two objects move toward each other:

\[
\boxed{
Time=\frac{Distance}{S_1+S_2}
}
\]

Example:

Two trains are 300 km apart.

Speeds:

\[
70,\ 80\ km/h
\]

Relative speed:

\[
150
\]

Time:

\[
300/150
\]

\[
\boxed{2\ hours}
\]


---

1️⃣4️⃣ Catch-Up Problems

If two objects move in the same direction:

\[
\boxed{
Time=\frac{Gap}{S_{fast}-S_{slow}}
}
\]

Example:

A is 20 km ahead.

A speed:

\[
40\ km/h
\]

B speed:

\[
60\ km/h
\]

Relative speed:

\[
60-40=20
\]

Time:

\[
20/20
\]

\[
\boxed{1\ hour}
\]


